  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms today and\nFriday.\n\nMultiple thunderstorm clusters will move from Pennsylvania east\nsoutheast through tonight along a frontal zone well defined on \nsurface observations and recent NUCAPS data. Given hot and \nunstable air mass south of this frontal zone and moderately \nstrong flow aloft, thunderstorms will be capable of producing \ndamaging wind gusts.\n\nA lot of uncertainty remains in timing and magnitude of\nconvection given recent CAMs haven't handled the extent of \nmorning showers and storms very well. General thought is that\nareas along and near the baroclinic zone left in the wake of\nprior convection will continue to see waves of showers and\nthunderstorms over the next 24 to 48 hours, with a gradual\nprogression south and west with each passing round.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions will persist through\nFriday.\n\nHave cancelled most of the Heat Advisory given lots of upstream\nclouds and convection. It will still be hot and humid nonetheless.\nSimilar heat indices are expected Friday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAs the cold front from earlier in the week tracks south of the \nregion, surface high pressure begins to move across the Great Lakes \nSaturday. Mid-level dry air settles over the Mid-Atlantic between \nSaturday and Sunday, allowing for heat & humidity to drop through \nthe weekend. Highs for Sunday range in the low 80s across much of \nthe area, with dewpoints ranging in the 50s and low-60s. Overall, it \nshould feel relatively mild & dry over the Labor Day holiday. \n\nThunderstorm chances drop during this period as well with \nincreasingly stable conditions. Looking towards early next week, \nupper-level ridging will begin to build over the Appalachians, \nallowing for relatively dry conditions to continue as temperatures \nstart to increase again by Tuesday. Thunderstorm chances remain low \nas this ridging builds overhead into next week.",
